 # Densification Strategy
 
-Also called: Gaussian Densification, Adaptive Density Control, Primitive Management
+Also called: Adaptive Density Control, Densification and Splitting, Primitive Management
 
 ## Overview
-Densification Strategy defines the mechanisms for dynamically managing the number and spatial distribution of 3D Gaussian primitives during the training of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) [Parent]. It addresses the problem of under-representing complex scene details or over-representing empty space by splitting, cloning, or pruning Gaussians based on optimization metrics [Parent]. What sets this family apart from static initialization methods is the iterative, feedback-driven adaptation of the primitive set throughout the training process, balancing reconstruction quality against computational cost [Parent].
+Densification strategies dynamically manage the cardinality and spatial distribution of 3D Gaussian primitives during the training of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) to balance reconstruction quality against computational cost [Parent]. They address the problem of under-representing complex scene details or over-representing empty space by iteratively splitting, cloning, or pruning Gaussians based on optimization metrics such as gradient magnitude, opacity, and scale [Parent]. What sets this family apart from static initialization methods is the feedback-driven adaptation of the primitive set throughout the training process, ensuring that the representation density matches the geometric complexity of the scene [Parent].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family operate on a set of 3D Gaussian primitives $\mathcal{G} = \{g_1, \dots, g_N\}$, where each $g_k$ is defined by a mean position $\mu_k \in \mathbb{R}^3$, a covariance matrix $\Sigma_k$ (parameterized by scale $s_k$ and rotation $q_k$), opacity $\alpha_k$, and spherical harmonic coefficients $\mathbf{c}_k$ [S8, unofficial].
